3418 is semaphore_create, and a reply for it must never be replayed

The guest caches MIG replies for a small list of message ids and replays them for later calls
to the same destination. The list was built for idempotent queries and 3418 was on it as a
task special port. It is not one: the generated server says
__DeclareRcvRpc(3418, "semaphore_create"), and every call to that must make a NEW semaphore
and hand back a NEW name.

Cached, the first reply came back for every later create, so libdispatch held one name for
three semaphores. The consequence is written in the comment directly below the list: a
replayed hit copies the NAME and adds no right. When the first holder disposed of it the name
died for all of them, and the next wait answered KERN_INVALID_NAME, which
_dispatch_sema4_timedwait traps on. That was the end of iTerm2 for this whole task.

Measured before: the daemon made two semaphores for the process and copied out 9731 and
21251, and the caller received 21251 three times and never saw 9731. Measured after: four
creates, four distinct names, no trap, and the application runs on to build its nine windows.

--- a/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/mach/impl/mach_traps.c
+++ b/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/mach/impl/mach_traps.c
@@ -161,7 +161,23 @@
 static libsimple_rwlock_t g_mach_msg_cache_lock = LIBSIMPLE_RWLOCK_INITIALIZER;
 
 static _Bool mach_msg_id_cacheable(mach_msg_id_t id) {
-	return id == 200 || id == 206 || id == 3409 || id == 3418;
+	/*
+	 * 3418 IS semaphore_create, NOT A TASK SPECIAL PORT, and caching it is why iTerm2 died.
+	 *
+	 * A reply may be replayed only for a routine that answers the same thing every time. The
+	 * comment on this list called 3418 a special-port query, and the generated server says
+	 * otherwise: __DeclareRcvRpc(3418, "semaphore_create"). Every call to that MUST make a new
+	 * semaphore and hand back a new name.
+	 *
+	 * Cached, the first reply was replayed for every later create, so libdispatch got one name for
+	 * three semaphores. The consequence is spelled out just below: a replayed hit copies the NAME
+	 * and adds no right, so when the first holder disposed of it the name died for all of them and
+	 * the next wait answered KERN_INVALID_NAME, which _dispatch_sema4_timedwait traps on.
+	 *
+	 * Measured: the daemon made two semaphores for that process and copied out two distinct names,
+	 * 9731 and 21251, and the caller received 21251 three times and never saw 9731.
+	 */
+	return id == 200 || id == 206 || id == 3409;
 }
 
